{"skill": "Exponential Word Problems", "difficulty": "Hard", "question": "Two samples of a radioactive substance are measured simultaneously at time t = 0. The first sample has 500 grams, and it decays by 12% every day. The second sample has 700 grams, and it decays by 8% every day. On which day will the difference between their masses first be less than 100 grams?

", "choices": ["A) 14", "B) 17", "C) 18", "D) 20"], "answer": "C", "explanation": "First, express each sample’s mass as a function of t, where t is the number of days after the initial measurement. The first sample, which begins at 500 grams and decays at a rate of 12% per day, can be modeled by 500(0.88)^t. The second sample, starting at 700 grams with an 8% decay rate, can be modeled by 700(0.92)^t.

We want the difference between the samples’ masses to be less than 100 grams for some integer t. That is, 700(0.92)^t − 500(0.88)^t < 100. Because solving this analytically can be complicated, we can evaluate the difference day by day. By substituting consecutive integer values of t, we find that on day 17, the difference is still slightly more than 100 grams, but on day 18, the difference falls below 100 grams. Therefore, the first day on which their masses differ by less than 100 grams is day 18.", "topic": "Exponential Decay Comparison"}

